WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-IT

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.159
How the economy works, in 30 minutes.

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:06.680
The economy works like a simple machine.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.390
But many people don't understand it

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.490
—or disagree with how it works

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.150
—and this has led to a lot of unnecessary economic suffering.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.249
I feel a deep responsibility

00:00:18.249 --> 00:00:22.049
to share my simple, yet practical economic model.

00:00:23.049 --> 00:00:25.320
Although unconventional, it

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.150
helped me predict and avoid the global financial crisis,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.460
and it has worked well for me for over 30 years.  Let's

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.260
begin.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.520
Although economics seems complex, it works in a simple, mechanical way.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.930
It is made up of a few simple parts and many simple transactions

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:47.260
that repeat themselves over and over again.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.460
These transactions are primarily driven by human nature

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.800
and create 3 main forces that drive the economy.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.120
Number 1: Productivity Growth

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.650
Number 2: Short-Term Debt Cycle

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.400
and Number 3: Long-Term Debt Cycle.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.770
We'll look at these three forces and how layering them together

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.530
creates a good model for tracking economic movements

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.590
and understanding what's happening right now.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.830
Let's start with the simplest part of economics:

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.860
transactions.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.840
An economy is nothing more than the sum of the transactions that constitute it,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.570
and a transaction is a very simple thing.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.360
Transactions are made continuously.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.760
Every time you buy something, you create a transaction.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.240
Each transaction consists of a buyer

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.150
exchanging money or credit

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:42.550
with a seller of goods, services, or financial assets.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.150
Credit acts as money,

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.280
so by adding together the money spent and the amount of credit used,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.160
you can figure out your total spending.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.560
The total amount spent pulls the economy.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.590
If you divide the amount spent

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.990
by the quantity sold,

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:01.290
you get the price.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.380
That's all.  This is a transaction.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.110
It is the basis of the economic machine.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:12.019
All cycles and all forces in an economy are driven by transactions.

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:14.469
So if we can understand transactions,

00:02:14.469 --> 00:02:16.569
we can understand the entire economy.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:19.510
A market consists of all the buyers

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:20.510
and sellers

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.910
transacting for the same thing.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.540
For example, there is a grain market,

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:26.840
a car market,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.640
a stock market,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.550
and markets for millions of other things.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.470
An economy consists of all the transactions

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.670
in all its markets.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.550
If you add up the total expenditure

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.450
and the total quantity sold

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.430
in all markets,

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.030
you have everything you need

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.630
to understand economics.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.960
It is extremely simple.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.430
People, corporations, banks, and governments

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:53.730
all engage in transactions in the manner described:

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.530
exchanging money and credit for goods, services, and financial assets.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.500
The largest buyer and seller is the public sector,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.040
which consists of two important parts:

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.690
a central government that collects taxes and spends money…

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.669
…and a central bank,

00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:12.269
which is different from other buyers and sellers because it

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:15.989
controls the amount of money and credit in the economy.  It does this by

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:19.650
manipulating interest rates

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.020
and printing new money.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.620
For these reasons, as we will see,

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.200
the central bank is an important player in the flow

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.700
of credit.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.629
I want you to be careful about credit.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:34.659
Credit is the most important part of the economy

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:36.539
and probably the least understood.

00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:40.319
It is the most important part because it is the

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.100
largest and most volatile part.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.100
Just as buyers and sellers go to the market to transact, so

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.969
do creditors and debtors.

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:53.569
People who lend money usually want to turn what they have into more money,

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:57.260
and people who go into debt usually want something they can't afford,

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.059
like a house or a car,

00:03:59.059 --> 00:04:02.319
or they want to invest in something like starting a business.

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:04.599
Credit can help both lenders

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:07.799
and borrowers get what they want.

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:11.560
Borrowers promise to repay the amount borrowed,

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.719
called principal,

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:15.419
plus an additional amount called interest.

00:04:15.419 --> 00:04:17.560
When interest rates are high,

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.560
people borrow less because it is expensive.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.540
When interest rates are low,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.040
people borrow more because it costs less.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.930
When debtors promise to repay

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:29.959
and creditors believe them,

00:04:29.959 --> 00:04:31.059
credit is created.

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:35.170
Two people can agree to create credit out of thin air!

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:39.210
This seems simple enough, but credit is difficult,

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.499
because it has different names.

00:04:40.499 --> 00:04:43.599
As soon as credit is created, it

00:04:43.599 --> 00:04:45.029
immediately converts into debt.

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:48.670
Debt is both an asset for the lender

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.770
and a liability for the borrower.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.540
In the future,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:56.240
when the borrower repays the loan plus interest,

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.549
the asset and liability disappear

00:04:58.549 --> 00:05:00.949
and the transaction is closed.

00:05:01.749 --> 00:05:04.629
So why is credit so important?

00:05:05.229 --> 00:05:07.619
Because when a debtor receives credit, his

00:05:07.619 --> 00:05:09.719
spending capacity increases.

00:05:09.719 --> 00:05:13.349
And remember, spending drives the economy.

00:05:13.349 --> 00:05:15.699
This happens because one person's spending

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:17.099
is another person's income.

00:05:17.499 --> 00:05:22.189
Think about it, every dollar you spend, someone earns.

00:05:22.189 --> 00:05:25.279
and every dollar you earn, someone spent.

00:05:25.279 --> 00:05:29.149
So when you spend more, someone earns more.

00:05:29.149 --> 00:05:32.649
When someone's income goes up,

00:05:32.649 --> 00:05:34.649
lenders are more inclined to lend them money

00:05:34.649 --> 00:05:37.629
because they now deserve more credit.

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:40.929
A debtor with good credit has two things:

00:05:40.929 --> 00:05:43.519
the ability to repay and collateral.

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:48.319
Having a lot of income in relation to his debt gives him the ability to repay.

00:05:49.079 --> 00:05:55.749
If he can't repay, he has marketable assets of value to use as collateral.

00:05:55.749 --> 00:06:00.340
This puts lenders at ease when lending money.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.960
So a higher income allows for greater debt,

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:05.680
which allows for increased spending.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.920
And since one person's spending is another person's income,

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:13.360
this leads to increasing debt and so on.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.350
This self-reinforcing model brings economic growth,

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.640
and that's why we have cycles.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.620
In a transaction you have to give something to get something.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.830
In the long run, how much you receive depends on how much you produce.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.430
Over time we learn and that accumulated knowledge increases

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.680
our standards of living.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.930
We call this productivity growth.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:40.430
The inventive and industrious increase

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.540
productivity and living standards faster

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.950
than the indolent and lazy,

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:49.710
but this is not always true in the short term.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:54.340
Productivity is especially important in the long term, but credit is especially important in the short term.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.370
This is because productivity growth does not fluctuate much,

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.480
so it is not a driver of large economic swings.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:06.750
Debt exists – because it allows us to consume more than we produce when we acquire it

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:11.050
and makes us consume less than we produce when we repay it.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:14.770
Its fluctuations occur in two major cycles.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:20.830
One takes about 5-8 years and the other about 75-100 years.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:25.160
Most people feel the fluctuations, but they typically don't see them as cycles,

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.490
because they see them too closely -- day by day.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.950
In this chapter we take a step back and look at these three great forces

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:37.000
and how they interact to shape our experiences.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:42.340
As mentioned, swings along this line aren't due to how much innovation or hard work there is,

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:46.240
they're mostly due to how much credit there is.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.070
Let's imagine for a moment an economy without credit.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.810
In this economy, the only way to increase my spending

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.480
is to increase my income,

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:59.080
which requires me to be more productive and do more work.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:03.020
Increased productivity is the only way to grow.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.250
Since my spending is income for someone else,

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:10.430
the economy grows whenever I or someone else is more productive.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:13.530
If we follow the transactions and let them unfold,

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.910
we see a progression like the productivity growth line.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:20.780
But since we go into debt, there are cycles.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.900
This is not due to any law,

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:27.660
but to human nature and the way credit works.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.270
Think of borrowing as an easy way to boost spending.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:37.479
To have something you can't afford, you have to spend more than you earn.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:41.460
To do this, you essentially have to go into debt with yourself in the future.

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.610
By doing this, you create a period in the future

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.690
where you will spend less than you earn to pay it off.  It

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.950
soon takes on the appearance of a cycle.  Simply put

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.400
, every time you go into debt you create a cycle.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:59.950
This is true for an individual and for the economy.

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:01.850
This is why understanding credit is so important,

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.590
because it sets in motion

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:08.790
a mechanical and predictable series of events that will occur in the future.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:11.570
This makes credit different from money.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:14.580
Currency is what you close transactions with.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:18.120
When you buy a beer from a bartender with cash,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.800
the transaction is closed immediately.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:22.630
But when you buy it on credit,

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:24.730
it's like opening a bar tab.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.530
You say you promise to pay in the future.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.070
Together, you and the barista create an asset and a liability.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.250
Now you have created credit out of thin air.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.900
Until you pay the bill,

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.250
the assets and liabilities don't disappear,

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.550
the debt doesn't go away,

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:44.090
and the transaction doesn't close.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:50.250
The reality is that most of what people call money is credit.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:55.510
The total amount of credit in the United States is about $50 trillion,

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:59.680
and the total amount of money is only about $3 trillion.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:02.040
Remember, in a creditless economy

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.200
the only way to increase your spending is to produce more.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.280
But in a credit economy,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:10.780
you can also increase your spending by borrowing.

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:14.120
As a result, a credit-rich economy has more spending

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:18.090
and allows incomes to rise faster than productivity in the short term,

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.890
but not in the long term.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:21.360
Now, don't get me wrong,

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:25.660
credit isn't always a bad thing that just causes cycles.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:30.900
It is bad when it inflates consumption that cannot be repaid.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:34.490
However, it is good when you allocate resources efficiently

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.690
and generate income to repay the debt.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:40.980
For example, if you go into debt to buy a large TV, you

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.879
don’t generate income to repay the debt.

00:10:44.079 --> 00:10:47.880
But, if you take out a loan for a tractor --

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.560
and that tractor lets you grow a bigger crop and earn more money

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:53.090
-- then you can pay off your debt

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:56.490
and improve your standard of living.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:57.600
In a credit economy,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.600
we can follow transactions

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:01.300
and see how credit creates growth.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.680
Let me give you an example.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.190
Suppose you earn $100,000 a year and have no debt.

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.540
You have enough credit to borrow $10,000

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:13.040
—say, from a credit card

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.250
—so you can spend $110,000

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:18.250
even though you only earn $100,000.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:21.460
Since your expense is someone else's income,

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:24.460
someone earns $110,000.

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:27.670
The person who earns $110,000

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:31.429
with no debt can borrow $11,000,

00:11:31.429 --> 00:11:34.070
so he can spend $121,000,

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:38.170
even though he only earned $110,000.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.640
His spending is someone else's income,

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.430
and by following the transactions

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:45.430
we see how this process

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.430
works in a self-reinforcing model.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:51.600
But remember, borrowing creates cycles,

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.809
and if the cycle goes up, sooner or later it has to go down.

00:11:56.009 --> 00:11:59.710
This brings us to the short-term debt cycle.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:04.380
As activity increases, we see an expansion

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.840
– the first phase of the short-term debt cycle.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.720
Spending continues to increase and prices are starting to rise.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.230
This happens because the increase in spending is fueled by credit

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.450
– which can be created instantly out of thin air.

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:22.680
When spending and incomes grow faster than the production of goods,

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:23.880
prices rise.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:28.000
When prices rise, we talk about inflation.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.500
The central bank doesn't want too much inflation,

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.010
because it causes problems.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:38.250
Seeing prices rising, he raises interest rates.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:42.959
With higher interest rates, fewer people can afford to borrow.

00:12:42.959 --> 00:12:45.600
And the cost of existing debt is rising.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:50.100
Think about it when your monthly credit card payments go up.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.459
As people borrow less and have higher debt repayments,

00:12:54.459 --> 00:12:58.720
they have less money left to spend, so spending decreases

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.779
… and since one person's spending is another person's income,

00:13:02.779 --> 00:13:06.589
income decreases… and so on.

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:10.800
When people spend less, prices go down.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.689
We call this deflation.

00:13:12.689 --> 00:13:17.460
Economic activity decreases and we have a recession.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:20.060
If the recession becomes too severe

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:22.440
and inflation is no longer a problem,

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:27.060
the central bank will lower interest rates to make everything rise again.

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:29.650
With low interest rates,

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:30.650
debt repayments are reduced

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:33.250
and borrowing and spending pick up

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:35.450
and we see another expansion.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:39.450
As you can see, the economy works like a machine.

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:44.160
In the short-term debt cycle, spending is limited only by the willingness of

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:47.140
creditors and debtors to give and receive credit.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:52.510
When credit is readily available, there is economic expansion.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:56.090
When credit is not readily available, there is a recession.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:00.450
And note that this cycle is mainly controlled by the central bank.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:05.120
The short-term debt cycle usually lasts 5-8 years

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:08.410
and repeats itself over and over for decades.

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:10.410
But notice that the base and

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.400
peak of each cycle end

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:16.670
with more growth than the previous cycle and with more debt.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.040
Why?

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.740
Because people push it

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:25.530
- they have a tendency to borrow and spend more, rather than repay.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:27.480
It's human nature.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.380
Because of this,

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:30.680
in the long run

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:33.470
debts increase faster than incomes,

00:14:33.470 --> 00:14:36.670
creating the long-term debt cycle.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:39.670
Even as people become more indebted,

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:44.030
lenders are also extending credit more freely.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.030
Why?

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:48.670
Because everyone thinks things are going great!

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:51.970
People only focus on the latest events.

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:55.650
And what happened lately?

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.650
Incomes have increased!

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.250
Asset values ​​are rising!

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.550
The stock market is roaring!  It

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:02.480
's a boom!  It is

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:06.690
worth purchasing goods, services and financial assets

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:08.390
with borrowed money!

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:11.770
When people overdo it, it's called a bubble.

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:14.640
So, even as debts have been growing,

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.110
incomes have been growing almost fast enough to offset them.

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:21.970
We call the debt-to-income ratio the debt burden.

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:25.210
As long as incomes continue to rise,

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.040
the debt burden remains manageable.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.230
At the same time, asset values ​​are rising.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:33.230
People borrow huge sums of money

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:35.060
to buy assets as investments,

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:37.660
causing prices to rise even further.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:40.260
People feel rich.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:43.260
Thus, even with the accumulation of a lot of debt,

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:49.490
the increase in income and asset values ​​​​helps debtors enjoy credit for a long time.

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:52.530
But this, of course, cannot continue forever.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.250
And he doesn't.

00:15:54.250 --> 00:16:00.520
Over decades, debt burdens slowly increase, creating ever-increasing debt repayments.

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:05.400
At a certain point, debt repayments begin to grow faster than incomes,

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.240
forcing people to reduce their spending.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:12.200
And since one person's spending is another person's income,

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:14.500
incomes start to decline...

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:19.020
...which makes people less creditworthy and causes lending to decline.

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:22.020
Debt repayments continue to rise,

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:24.440
which leads to further declines in spending...

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:27.140
...and the cycle reverses.

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:30.960
This is peak long-term debt.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:34.150
Debt burdens have become too great.

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:38.830
For the United States, Europe, and much of the rest of the world, this

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:40.760
happened in 2008.

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:45.430
It happened for the same reason it happened in Japan in 1989

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.750
and in the United States in 1929.

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:51.980
Now the economy is beginning to deleverage.

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:56.500
In a deleveraging crisis, people cut spending,

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:59.540
incomes fall, credit disappears,

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:02.700
asset prices fall, banks come under pressure,

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.340
the stock market crashes, social tensions rise,

00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:09.709
and everything begins to feed itself in the other direction.

00:17:09.709 --> 00:17:13.850
As incomes fall and debt repayments rise,

00:17:13.850 --> 00:17:17.990
debtors are under pressure.  No longer creditworthy,

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:22.360
the credit lapses and debtors can no longer borrow money to

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:23.600
repay the debt.

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:28.440
Struggling to fill this hole, debtors are having to sell off assets.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.550
The rush to sell goods is taking over the market.

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:36.370
This is when the stock market crashes,

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:39.740
the housing market tanks, and the banks run into trouble.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:44.940
As the prices of goods fall, the value of the collateral that the debtor can offer decreases.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:48.280
This makes debtors even less creditworthy.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:50.200
People feel poor.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:55.010
Credit disappears quickly.  Less spending ›

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:55.890
less income ›

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:57.890
less wealth ›

00:17:57.890 --> 00:17:58.590
less credit ›

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:00.800
less loans and so on.

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:03.000
It's a vicious cycle.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.530
This appears similar to a recession, but the difference here

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:10.160
is that interest rates cannot be lowered to save the situation.

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:14.590
In a recession, lowering interest rates pushes people into debt.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:18.570
However, in a deleveraging scenario, lowering interest rates doesn't work because

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:20.530
interest rates are already

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:25.310
low and will soon hit 0%—and then the stimulus ends.

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:29.210
Interest rates in the United States hit 0% during the deleveraging of

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:30.840
the 1930s

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:33.440
and again in 2008.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.840
The difference between a recession

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:40.270
and a deleveraging is that in a recession, debt burdens are

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:41.930
simply magnified

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:45.050
and cannot be alleviated by lowering interest rates.

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:50.240
Lenders realize that the debts have become too large to be repaid in full.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:55.310
The debtors have lost their ability to repay and their collateral its value.  They

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:59.370
feel paralyzed by debt – they don't want to know anything about it anymore!

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:03.490
Creditors stop lending.  Debtors stop borrowing.

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:07.020
Think of the economy as uncreditable,

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:09.000
just like an individual.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.600
So what do you do about deleveraging?

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:17.540
The problem is that debt burdens are too high and need to come down.

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:20.790
There are four ways this can happen.

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:24.489
1. People, businesses and governments cut their spending.

00:19:24.489 --> 00:19:28.409
2. Debts are reduced through bankruptcies and restructurings.

00:19:28.409 --> 00:19:34.139
3. Wealth is redistributed from the haves to the have-nots.  And finally

00:19:34.139 --> 00:19:37.980
4. the central bank prints new money.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:42.999
These 4 ways have happened in every deleveraging in modern history.

00:19:45.699 --> 00:19:47.779
Spending is usually cut first.

00:19:47.879 --> 00:19:52.129
As we have just seen, people, businesses, banks, and even governments are tightening their belts and

00:19:52.129 --> 00:19:54.869
cutting spending to pay off their debt.

00:19:54.869 --> 00:19:58.559
This is often referred to as austerity.

00:19:58.559 --> 00:20:01.830
When borrowers stop taking out new loans

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:06.769
and start paying off old debts, the debt burden is expected to decrease.

00:20:06.769 --> 00:20:10.720
But the opposite happens!  Because spending is cut

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:14.610
—and one person’s spending is another person’s income—it drives

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:19.029
down incomes.  They fall faster than debts are repaid

00:20:19.029 --> 00:20:23.210
and the debt burden actually worsens.  As we have seen,

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:26.330
this spending cut is deflationary and painful.

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.389
Companies are forced to cut costs...

00:20:29.389 --> 00:20:32.779
which means fewer jobs and higher unemployment.

00:20:32.779 --> 00:20:37.139
This leads to the next step: debts must be reduced!

00:20:37.139 --> 00:20:41.149
Many debtors cannot repay their debts

00:20:41.149 --> 00:20:44.299
- and a debtor's debts are a creditor's assets.

00:20:44.299 --> 00:20:48.749
When borrowers don't repay the bank, people think the bank can't

00:20:48.749 --> 00:20:50.309
repay them,

00:20:50.309 --> 00:20:54.909
so they rush to withdraw their money from the bank.  Banks are under pressure and

00:20:54.909 --> 00:20:55.590
people,

00:20:55.590 --> 00:21:00.080
businesses and banks are defaulting on their debts.  This severe

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:03.570
economic contraction is a depression.

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:08.649
Much of a depression consists of people discovering that much of what they thought

00:21:08.649 --> 00:21:11.089
was their wealth is no longer there.

00:21:11.089 --> 00:21:13.490
Let's go back to the bar.

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:17.619
When you bought a beer and put it on your tab at the bar,

00:21:17.619 --> 00:21:23.570
you promised to reimburse the bartender.  Your promise has become a barista's activity.

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:28.129
But if you break the promise—if you don't pay him back and default

00:21:28.129 --> 00:21:29.309
on your bar tab—

00:21:29.309 --> 00:21:32.649
then the "business" he has is effectively worthless.

00:21:32.649 --> 00:21:35.779
It's essentially disappeared.

00:21:35.779 --> 00:21:39.710
Many lenders do not want the businesses to disappear and accept

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:40.759
debt restructuring.

00:21:40.759 --> 00:21:44.320
Debt restructuring means that creditors are repaid

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:48.049
less or over a longer period of time

00:21:48.049 --> 00:21:52.330
or at a lower interest rate than previously agreed.  Somehow

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:56.889
the contract is breached to reduce the debt.  Lenders would rather have a

00:21:56.889 --> 00:21:59.419
little of something than all of nothing.

00:21:59.419 --> 00:22:03.450
Even if the debt disappears, debt restructuring causes

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:06.559
income and asset values ​​to disappear more rapidly,

00:22:06.559 --> 00:22:09.679
so the debt burden continues to worsen.

00:22:09.679 --> 00:22:12.950
Like cutting spending, debt reduction

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:16.009
is also painful and deflationary.

00:22:16.009 --> 00:22:21.940
All of this impacts the central government, because lower incomes and less employment

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:27.049
mean the government collects less taxes.

00:22:27.049 --> 00:22:30.210
At the same time, it needs to increase spending because unemployment has risen.

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:33.960
Many of the unemployed have inadequate savings

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.419
and need financial support from the government.

00:22:36.419 --> 00:22:39.999
Governments also create stimulus plans

00:22:39.999 --> 00:22:43.879
and increase their spending to compensate for the weakening economy.

00:22:43.879 --> 00:22:47.509
Government budget deficits explode in

00:22:47.509 --> 00:22:51.059
deleveraging because they spend more than they earn in taxes.

00:22:51.059 --> 00:22:55.720
This is what happens when you hear about the budget deficit on the news.

00:22:55.720 --> 00:23:01.320
To finance their deficits, governments must raise taxes

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:06.059
or borrow money.  But with declining incomes and so many unemployed, where does

00:23:06.059 --> 00:23:09.789
the money come from?  From the rich.

00:23:09.789 --> 00:23:14.580
Since governments need more money and wealth is highly concentrated in the

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:17.109
hands of a small percentage of people,

00:23:17.109 --> 00:23:20.379
governments naturally raise taxes on the wealthy,

00:23:20.379 --> 00:23:24.450
which facilitates a redistribution of wealth throughout the economy—

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:29.279
from the haves to the have-nots.  The have-nots, who are suffering, are beginning to

00:23:29.279 --> 00:23:30.970
dislike the have-nots.

00:23:30.970 --> 00:23:36.309
The wealthy, pressured by a weak economy, falling asset values,

00:23:36.309 --> 00:23:39.940
and higher taxes, reciprocate the antipathy toward the have-nots.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:43.890
If depression continues, social disorder may erupt.

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:47.280
Not only do tensions increase within countries,

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:52.200
but they can arise between countries – especially debtor and creditor countries.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:56.040
The situation can lead to political change

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:58.840
that can sometimes be extreme.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.630
In the 1930s this brought Hitler to power,

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:08.080
war in Europe and depression in the United States.  The pressure to do something

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.070
to stop the depression increases.

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:14.950
Remember, most of what people thought was money was actually credit.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:18.400
So when credit disappears, people don't have enough money.

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:23.190
People are desperate for money and remember who prints money?

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:27.370
The central bank.

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:30.309
Having already lowered interest rates to nearly 0

00:24:30.309 --> 00:24:34.090
- it is forced to print money.  Unlike spending cuts,

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:37.299
debt reduction, and wealth redistribution,

00:24:37.299 --> 00:24:42.190
printing money is inflationary and stimulative.  Inevitably, the central bank

00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:43.210
prints new money

00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:47.150
– out of thin air – and uses it to buy financial assets

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:52.260
and government bonds.  It happened in the United States during the Great Depression

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:56.010
and again in 2008, when the US central bank –

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:59.940
the Federal Reserve – printed over two trillion dollars.

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:04.179
Other central banks around the world that had the power to do so printed a lot of money.

00:25:04.179 --> 00:25:07.440
By purchasing financial goods with this currency,

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:11.679
you encourage the prices of goods to rise, which makes people more creditworthy.

00:25:11.679 --> 00:25:15.820
However, this only helps those who have financial assets.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:21.840
The central bank can print money, but it can only buy financial assets.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:24.900
The central government, on the other hand,

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:29.580
can purchase goods and services and put money into the hands of the people,

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:34.520
but it cannot print it.  So, in order to stimulate the economy, the two

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:35.650
must cooperate.

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:40.470
By buying government bonds, the central bank essentially lends money to the

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:41.190
government,

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.909
allowing it to run a deficit and increase spending

00:25:44.909 --> 00:25:48.650
on goods and services through its stimulus programs

00:25:48.650 --> 00:25:53.080
and unemployment benefits.  This increases public income

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:56.240
and government debt.  However,

00:25:56.240 --> 00:25:59.540
the economy's overall debt burden is decreasing.

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:05.429
This is a very risky time.  Policymakers must balance the four ways

00:26:05.429 --> 00:26:06.590
in which debt burdens decline.

00:26:06.590 --> 00:26:13.050
Deflationary methods must balance with inflationary methods

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:14.710
to maintain stability.

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:18.130
If balanced correctly, there can be a

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:21.479
nice deleveraging effect.

00:26:21.479 --> 00:26:25.359
Now deleveraging can be horrible or wonderful.

00:26:25.359 --> 00:26:28.839
How can deleveraging be beautiful?

00:26:28.839 --> 00:26:33.429
While deleveraging is a challenging situation,

00:26:33.429 --> 00:26:37.549
managing it as best as possible is wonderful.

00:26:37.549 --> 00:26:42.210
Much nicer than debt-fueled, unbalanced excesses of the

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:45.940
leverage phase.  In a successful deleveraging,

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:51.089
debt decreases relative to income, real economic growth is positive,

00:26:51.089 --> 00:26:56.889
and inflation is not a problem.  It was achieved with the right balance.

00:26:56.889 --> 00:27:00.159
The right balance requires some combination

00:27:00.159 --> 00:27:04.229
of spending cuts, debt reduction, wealth transfer,

00:27:04.229 --> 00:27:09.149
and money printing so that economic and social stability can be maintained.

00:27:09.149 --> 00:27:13.639
People ask whether printing money will increase inflation.

00:27:13.639 --> 00:27:18.879
No, if it compensates for the decline in credit.  Remember, the expense is what matters.

00:27:18.879 --> 00:27:24.249
A dollar of spending paid for with money has the same effect on price as a dollar

00:27:24.249 --> 00:27:25.969
of spending paid for with credit.

00:27:25.969 --> 00:27:31.299
By printing money, the central bank can compensate for the disappearance of credit

00:27:31.299 --> 00:27:33.080
with an increase in the quantity of money.

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:38.509
To change things, the central bank needs not only to stimulate

00:27:38.509 --> 00:27:39.519
income growth,

00:27:39.519 --> 00:27:43.849
but to increase the rate of income growth more than the interest rate on

00:27:43.849 --> 00:27:45.099
accumulated debt.

00:27:45.099 --> 00:27:48.379
So what do I mean by this?  Basically,

00:27:48.379 --> 00:27:52.309
income needs to grow faster than debt.  For example:

00:27:52.309 --> 00:27:56.080
Suppose a country undergoing deleveraging has a debt-

00:27:56.080 --> 00:27:58.580
to-income ratio of 100%.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:03.659
This means that the amount of debt it has is the same as the amount of income

00:28:03.659 --> 00:28:05.799
the entire country produces in a year.

00:28:05.799 --> 00:28:09.009
Now think about the interest rate on this debt,

00:28:09.009 --> 00:28:11.159
let's say it's 2%.

00:28:11.159 --> 00:28:15.029
If your debt rises to 2% because of this interest rate and

00:28:15.029 --> 00:28:15.690
your income

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:20.230
only rises about 1%, you will never be able to reduce your debt burden.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:24.809
Enough money needs to be printed to push the rate of income growth above the

00:28:24.809 --> 00:28:25.950
interest rate.

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:30.720
However, money printing is easy to abuse because it is so instant and

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:33.049
people prefer it to alternatives.

00:28:33.049 --> 00:28:36.200
The key is to avoid printing too much money

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:41.110
and triggering rampant inflation, as Germany did during

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:43.129
deleveraging in the 1920s.

00:28:43.129 --> 00:28:48.269
If policymakers strike the right balance, deleveraging isn't that dramatic.

00:28:48.269 --> 00:28:51.340
Growth is slow, but debt burdens are falling.

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:54.470
That's a nice deleveraging.

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:59.830
As incomes rise, debtors begin to look more creditworthy.

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:02.999
And when borrowers appear more creditworthy,

00:29:02.999 --> 00:29:08.509
lenders start lending again.  Debt burdens are finally coming down.

00:29:08.509 --> 00:29:13.499
By being able to borrow money, people can spend more.  Eventually, the economy begins to

00:29:13.499 --> 00:29:14.399
grow again,

00:29:14.399 --> 00:29:18.009
leading to the reflation phase of the long-term debt cycle.

00:29:18.009 --> 00:29:22.490
While the deleveraging process can be horrific, if managed poorly,

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.279
if managed well, it will eventually solve the problem.

00:29:26.279 --> 00:29:29.580
It takes about a decade or more

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:33.669
for debt burdens to decline and economic activity to return to normal

00:29:33.669 --> 00:29:37.899
– hence the term “lost decade.”

00:29:37.899 --> 00:29:42.720
Of course, the economy is a bit more complicated than this model

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:43.639
suggests.

00:29:43.639 --> 00:29:48.850
However, placing the short-term debt cycle on top of the long-term debt cycle

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:52.629
and then placing both above the productivity growth line

00:29:52.629 --> 00:29:55.919
gives a reasonably good model for seeing where we have been,

00:29:55.919 --> 00:29:58.919
where we are now, and where we are likely going.

00:29:58.919 --> 00:30:03.019
In summary, there are three rules of thumb I would like you to take away

00:30:03.019 --> 00:30:03.720
from this.

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:08.220
First, don't let your debt rise faster than your income,

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:11.429
because your debt burdens will eventually crush you.

00:30:11.429 --> 00:30:16.059
Second, don't let income rise faster than productivity,

00:30:16.059 --> 00:30:19.159
because you will eventually become uncompetitive.

00:30:19.159 --> 00:30:24.000
And third: do everything you can to increase productivity,

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:29.029
because, in the long run, that's what matters most.

00:30:29.549 --> 00:30:34.080
This is simple advice for you and policy makers.

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:38.470
You might be surprised, but most people – including the majority of policymakers – don't pay enough attention

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:39.509
to this.

00:30:39.509 --> 00:30:44.059
This model worked for me and I hope it will work for you.

00:30:44.059 --> 00:30:46.380
Thank you.

